CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts _ --no-standard-checks
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* x is 0 or 1: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^\[.*\.assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^\[.*\.loop_assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^Invariant check failed$
--
DFCC instruments first (declared implicitly, after second) before second.
To infer the assigns clause of the loop of second, inlining reaches the body
of first, which is instrumented and so passes the write set as the first
argument to set_x, which is not instrumented yet. That argument has no
parameter, and set_x's parameter v got &x, which stopped the inliner. The
loops havoc x, so the assertion after them fails.
